%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
I decided to take this course after my friend told me about it, and I found the concept of designing technology to aid people with dementia really intriguing. In my Computer Science studies I often times feel like the focus is on rather abstract concepts and applications. I am used to highly analytical courses that present algorithms or methods and how to apply them on similar, specific problems. So, in that sense, having the opportunity to do a course that focused on something rather tangible, with so much flexibility and freedom in choosing what we want to create, felt refreshing and exciting. Of course, it was not the first course I took that focused more on the HCI part of computer science, but I really enjoyed the concept behind it. 

As for the SCE process itself, I have to admit that it was really something new for me. It felt structured and organized, and every section had its own role, place, and categorization. There were concepts I was familiar with, such as the Stakeholder, Problem Scenario, Technology, and Evaluation. However, I had never before really worked through how they are all connected together, what influences what, and I had never written that down in a guided, structural way as the XWiki format requires. I also felt like the sensitive nature of our product, which aimed to help a rather vulnerable group of people, made the motivation behind this level of structure very clear. It justified having so many detailed sections and specifications for every decision and design step.

Something that I learned that I found very important and interesting, but have never considered in depth before, was Value Tensions. How we want to create a product that fulfills a certain role or goal while also finding some balance with possible conflicts and implications. I also realized that every design decision, step, and consideration should be rooted in either literature or experience. Even though now this feels very self-explanatory and logical, I just had never thought about it that much before.

One thing I have noticed is that, since there are so many little sections, subsections, and instances that we have written for the XWiki, it can become a little difficult to see everything in the grand scheme. And then I realized that, while it may not be the first place I would look at when reading through a report like the one we are submitting, the Graph Edges specifications really offer a meaningful overview of how everything influences everything and how every single part is meaningful within the overall system. 

Overall, I felt like this course offered a very solid structure and methodology for designing our system while also allowing a very large amount of creativity and freedom in our choices, decisions, and overall process.

Throughout the course I tried to join every lecture. I especially enjoyed the lecture on Memory, as it showed insights of how our brains work and how that can be mapped to methods and technologies to help with fulfilling specific goals and needs. I also liked learning about what a Cognitive Walkthrough is, and I enjoyed the entire Evaluation lecture and overall process. 
While it was a lot of work, it helped to have a very well defined structure and list of expectations for each deliverable and each step of the process. As for my personal contribution, I wrote the Design Scenario, and Personas and Profiles sections for the Specification part in the first half of the course. I was quite active in determining how we should organize ourselves as a team, with meetings, tasks distribution, and general workflow. I realized that I am at my best when there is a concrete action plan and when it is crystal clear what everyone is doing, and that I like ensuring that. I was also involved with making the slides and presenting the midterm presentation, and later on helped with feedback and proofreading the report, the video, and the final presentation. For the second part of the course, I have to admit that I could have been more involved, and that I had not realized how much time my jobs, other courses, and extracurricular commitments would take. I still helped with the evaluation part, during the lab where we tested our prototypes with other teams, and I tried to give input and help whenever asked. 
If I could go back and start over, I would probably try to balance the workload better, so that I can have a consistent level of involvement throughout the course.

The teamwork itself went smoothly for the most part, we were able to communicate and find compromises when our schedules or meeting styles did not align. Another thing that happened that we successfully navigated - I would not explicitly call it a tension, but more like a moment of imbalance - was when we decided to completely switch our project goal and purpose after the midterm presentation. We got over the initial differences in opinions regarding this decision, and acted together to put our ideas into application. So for all of that I am very grateful for my team! It was really lovely working together with them.